WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  these men and women make a part of the  largest haitian expat community in latin  america  after their country's 2010 earthquake  chile opened its doors to tens of  thousands of haitians  but today even as thousands of  undocumented venezuelans colombians and  peruvians are flocking to chile  huge numbers of haitians are actually  leaving on the u.s mexico border  hundreds of haitians have discarded  their chilean id cards  as they risk their lives trying to reach  u.s shores  the question is why would they leave  chile the country with the highest per  capita income in the region  the treatment that haitians receive here  from chile's current president is  indecent that's why they decide to  journey to el dorado even if they die on  the way because they are sure they will  be treated better  the death of a young haitian mother  shook the community to the core  two years ago twenty-seven-year-old  joanne florville who did not speak  spanish was arrested and later taken  from the police station to the hospital  in a coma  her sister says that floorbill had been  accused of abandoning her two-month-old  baby at a municipal office when in fact  she had only left her in her stroller  with a guard for a few minutes while she  tried to find someone to translate for  her  samantha agreed to speak to us for the  first time about what her sister told  her before she died a month later  including charges that she was badly  beaten by police  orlando that's why she cried so much she  told me the police and others treat  haitians badly here she said they treat  haitians like dogs but actually that's  not true because here they treat dogs  quite well  samantha nevertheless wants to remain in  chile to be near her deceased sister's  child who lives with her father  she adds that not all chileans are bad  such as roxanna her brother's girlfriend  but racism and discrimination of  haitians are widespread  in chile many associate haitians with  compassion they are seen as people who  are socially degraded defenseless  inferior in some ways but racism isn't  exclusively about the color of one's  skin discrimination is based on class  it's poverty that makes the difference  at a migrant assistance center in  santiago we meet another haitian migrant  who asked not to be identified for fear  of reprisals  she says the main problem isn't racism  it's the government's refusal to issue  or renew working documents for most  haitians  if you want to work you have to have an  id card and to have an id card you need  to work so if you can't get papers how  can you work you can't live decently  that's why the majority of my friends  have left the country i have no friends  left  approximately 90 percent of haitians in  chile don't have valid residency papers  which makes them open to rampant abuse  from landlords and unscrupulous  employers and ineligible for social  services  chile's current government has become  more hostile towards undocumented  migrants especially those who for  example are working in the informal  sector critics say that it has done  little or nothing to promote social  inclusion nor to crack down on illegal  exploitation of patients all of which is  keeping them at the bottom of the social  and economic ladder in this country  and until that changes the majority of  haitians who came here thinking they had  reached the promised land will continue  to live in squalor and suffer  discrimination  lucia newman al jazeera santiago

